result.summary:
I checked every version against Maven Central, Google Maven, the Gradle plugin portal and the GitHub Releases API on 2026-10-08, and read the published source jars of Kyant backdrop 2.0.1, shapes 1.2.1, navigation3-ui 1.2.0, Compose UI/foundation/animation 1.12.1 and Haze 2.0.1. The template is mostly current. It is behind on Navigation3 (1.1.7 → 1.2.0, stable 2026-09-23), Kotlin (2.4.20 → 2.4.21), WorkManager (2.12.0), Paging (3.5.2), core-ktx (1.19.1), Kover (0.9.11), Firebase BoM (35.0.0, a major release; minSdk 24 is fine) and the Gradle wrapper (9.7.1 → 9.8.1). check_versions.py cannot see the Gradle wrapper or new artifact groups such as androidx.room3 (Room 3.0.3, stable since 2026-07-01). The template also runs AGP 9.4.1, above the newest AGP KGP 2.4.20 is documented as tested with (9.3.1).

Kyant backdrop has been Kotlin Multiplatform since 2.0.0 (android, desktop, iOS, macOS, js, wasm). Blur and vibrancy need API 31+, and lens refraction and the highlight shader need API 33+. Below API 31 it draws a sharp, unblurred copy of the background, so a frosted fallback is needed (template minSdk is 26). lens() throws for any shape that isn't rounded-rectangular, but only on API 33+. A node that samples a backdrop from inside that backdrop's own recorded content crashes the RenderThread.

The library source shows where the cost is. Every drawBackdrop adds its own offscreen layer and RenderEffect, and the default highlight and shadow add two more re-recorded layers. The effects lambda is snapshot-observed, so animating values inside it rebuilds the effect chain every frame. That is exactly what PeriFlow's glass bottom bar indicator does today. The cheap path:
- one recorded scene per screen;
- real glass only on floating chrome, with content-level glass drawn instead (PeriFlow already measured this win);
- static effects;
- press animation through layerBlock;
- highlight and shadow turned off where not seen;
- Compose 1.12's cached Modifier.dropShadow instead of PeriFlow's per-draw setShadowLayer.

Haze 2.0.1 is the alternative: better fallbacks, benchmarks and accessibility settings, but its glass API is still @ExperimentalHazeApi.

For transitions, Compose slides are placement-only and cheap, but fading a whole screen (alpha below 1) forces a full-screen offscreen buffer. The template's push fades the outgoing screen on a tween while the slide runs on a spring, and its tab switch fades and scales two full screens after a delay. Navigation3 defaults to a 700 ms fade. The recommended iOS-style recipe:
- 100% slide for the incoming screen, 30% parallax for the outgoing one, on the same spring;
- a draw-phase scrim drawn by the top screen instead of alpha (no offscreen buffer);
- instant tab switching.

iOS springs convert exactly: stiffness = (2π/duration)² and dampingRatio = 1 − bounce, from Apple's Spring documentation. Compose foundation has no ripple; the template already supplies its own indication through LocalIndication, which is the right mechanism.

For the Compose Multiplatform generator, the 2026 layout is a shared umbrella module, a separate androidApp (required by AGP 9) and an iosApp. Shared modules use the com.android.kotlin.multiplatform.library plugin with the kotlin { android {} } block (androidLibrary {} is deprecated since AGP 9.1); that plugin has no build variants and no BuildConfig. KSP 2.3.10+ deprecates ksp(...) in KMP projects. Libraries: JetBrains navigation3-ui 1.1.2, which needs polymorphic NavKey serialisation on non-JVM targets; Koin 4.2.2 with its compiler plugin 1.2.1, or Metro 1.4.5; SKIE 0.10.15.

For the SwiftUI generator: @Observable, NavigationStack(path:), local Swift packages, XcodeGen 2.46.0 with syncedFolder sources and local packages, and Swift Testing. The Liquid Glass APIs (glassEffect, GlassEffectContainer, .buttonStyle(.glass)) need iOS 26, and the UIDesignRequiresCompatibility opt-out is ignored when building for iOS 27.

Unverified items are listed in §6 of the report.
